00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.500
China is huge.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.870
The kind of huge that’s hard to wrap your
head around.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:19.659
Beginning in the 1950’s, its population
exploded, from an already respectable 500

00:00:19.659 --> 00:00:22.999
million to almost three times that today.

00:00:22.999 --> 00:00:28.339
which makes it bigger than all of North America,
Australia, and Europe combined.

00:00:28.339 --> 00:00:32.439
Its consistent economic growth has made it
one of the world’s great powers,

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:38.340
with enough military might to claim the strategically
important South China Sea,

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:43.470
and enough influence to begin the most ambitious
infrastructure project in history.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:50.690
A $1 trillion dollar network of ports, pipelines,
and railroads across 65 countries.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:52.940
But none of this was inevitable.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:59.280
While China rapidly and forcefully industrialized,
it faced massive famine and housing shortages.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.080
Its economy needed time to develop, and the
world deeply feared overpopulation.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.610
China’s response was the famous One Child
Policy, which limited ethnic Chinese families

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.640
to a single child, with a few exceptions.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.440
To enforce the law, women were forcefully
sterilized and fined for having too many children.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.690
The problem is: it worked.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.050
Or, something did.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:28.450
Historians doubt it prevented all 400 million
births claimed by the government, But China’s

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:33.040
Total Fertility Rate, the average number of
children a woman will have in her lifetime,

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.100
has fallen all the way to 1.6,

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.680
well below the 2.1 needed to maintain its
size.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:46.310
There simply aren’t enough children, and
in a few short years, China will begin to

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.310
shrink.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:53.150
The One Child Policy was repealed in 2015,
but it won’t make a significant difference,

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:58.920
because it only ever sped-up the unavoidable:
As nations develop, they choose to have drastically

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.350
fewer children.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:05.750
China’s problem isn’t unique, about half
the world lives in a country that is or soon

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:12.310
will be in the same position, but it is uniquely
big, and the timing, uniquely bad.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:19.250
The story of China in the 21st century is
just as much about demographics as it is GDP,

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:25.190
military power, the rule of Xi Jinping, all
of which will be seriously tested by the coming

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:26.870
demographic crisis.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:31.570
To understand why it’s such a threat, and
whether something can be done, we need to

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.950
look a little more closely.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.060
As individuals, humans are unpredictable.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:44.160
We don’t know what someone will do, or say,
or buy, because they don’t know.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.370
Impulse guides your decision to add guacamole
just as it does what college you attend.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:53.690
But, countries don’t care about individuals,
they care about groups.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:57.819
And the beauty of demography is that groups
are predictable.

00:02:57.819 --> 00:02:58.819
Very.

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:03.980
Of course, nothing is certain, theories compete
and estimates vary, but it’s much easier

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:08.690
to guess how many 18-years-olds we’ll have
in 30 years and in general, what they’ll

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:14.069
be doing than, say, the next three decades
of foreign policy or culture.

00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:19.960
No country has yet figured out how to manufacture
18 year olds, not even China, and that means

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:24.520
population today is a good peek at population
tomorrow.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:31.280
When this information is combined with geopolitics
or economics, it goes from mildly interesting

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.800
to downright powerful.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.599
Here’s what we know about China:

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:40.190
Each of these lines is one of its age groups,
with babies at the bottom, and elderly at

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:41.190
the top.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:42.750
First, are consumers.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:49.349
From 18 to 45, we know people are spending
- they’re going to school, taking out loans,

00:03:49.349 --> 00:03:51.220
saving… not so much.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:56.460
and despite what this group says about millennials,
they’re very important, because consumer

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:00.150
spending is one of the biggest contributors
to economic growth.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.239
Next, are the money makers.

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:06.470
These people have paid off their debt, now
they’re saving for retirement.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:10.629
And even though they’re a smaller share
of the population, they generate most of its

00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:11.629
income.

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:16.180
In the U.S., for example, they alone pay half
of all income tax.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:18.630
That makes them, a government’s best friend.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:24.720
Finally, at age 65, people are done working,
done saving, and, largely, done spending.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.280
What’s special about this group, is how
quickly and how dramatically it begins:

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:36.290
In a single day, a retiree often goes from
contributing the highest taxes of their lifetime,

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:41.139
to almost nothing, as they slowly collect
pensions and social security.

00:04:41.139 --> 00:04:44.600
For right now, let’s ignore the total number
of people.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:48.200
China could be bigger like this, or smaller
like this,

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:52.630
What’s important is the balance between
these different groups, and that’s why this

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.190
graphic is so useful.

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:59.260
It’s called a Population Pyramid, because,
for most of history, it has been.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:04.340
A constant stream of babies at the bottom,
and a small number of deaths with each subsequent

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:05.340
year.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:10.009
A good example is Niger, where the average
woman has 6.5 children.

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:14.000
Mortality is very high, making the average
age only 15.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.169
But much of the world no longer looks like
a pyramid.

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:19.720
In China, it’s turning upside down.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:25.050
As you can see, there are two big bulges in
its population, here and here.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.870
The first is currently in its peak spending
years.

00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:32.030
The second, right in the prime of its high-earning,
high-tax-contributing years.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:36.220
It’s no wonder China is seeing massive economic
growth.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:42.139
But that’s what makes a demographic crisis
such an ugly one: it happens verrryyyy slowly,

00:05:42.139 --> 00:05:43.850
and then, all at once.

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:49.030
Remember, this huge groups of workers will
soon, and quite suddenly retire, as they start

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:51.040
waiting for the checks to arrive.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.390
But the group responsible for writing those
checks, or at least, funding them, is getting

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:57.140
smaller and smaller.

00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:02.400
The problem isn’t just financial, A single
child must now care for two parents and four

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:03.690
grandparents.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:08.789
The United Nations expects China’s Dependency
Ratio, the number of non-working compared

00:06:08.789 --> 00:06:14.160
to working-age people, to increase at roughly
the same rate as Japan’s, whose population

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.949
began shrinking in 2011, and now sells more
adult diapers than infant ones.

00:06:19.949 --> 00:06:26.380
By 2050, China may have more retirees than
all of Germany, Japan, France, and Britain.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:31.710
Worse, the One Child Policy, combined with
a cultural preference for males, has created

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.970
a massive gender imbalance.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:39.360
As a result, it’s likely that by 2030, one-fourth
of Chinese men in their late 30’s will have

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:40.740
never married.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:46.120
At a minimum, an abundance of forgotten young
men will cause some social anxiety.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:50.180
Or possibly, as some experts suggest, serious
conflict.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:52.620
It sounds a lot like the plot of a movie.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:54.750
Perhaps, “No Country for Young Men”

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:59.900
Of course, China is aware of the problem,
But it’s fighting an inevitable demographic

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:01.080
transition.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:05.520
In the beginning, For China, the early 20th
century, children are abundant.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:10.639
Because: you can only expect a few to survive,
you don’t have the education or tools for

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:16.580
family planning, and because the best way
to grow tomatoes is to first grow children.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:17.580
Seriously.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:22.319
For any sleep-deprived parents watching, this
will be a shock, but giving birth was once

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:24.450
the ultimate productivity hack.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:27.889
Before there were tractors, there were children.

00:07:27.889 --> 00:07:29.990
And then, people stop dying.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:35.580
It really only takes a few improvements to
healthcare for rapid reductions in mortality.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:42.300
And that’s how the world grew from 1.6 to
6.1 billion people in one century,

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:47.410
That short window where fewer people are dying,
but everyone’s still having children.

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:53.300
But it is just a window, after mortality drops,
fertility is right behind it.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:58.639
As industrialization brings rural workers
to find jobs in the city, Children become

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:04.419
less a utility and more a liability - the
kind that screams, and cries, and generates

00:08:04.419 --> 00:08:05.419
student loan debt.

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:10.020
As the saying goes: the best contraceptive
is economic development.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:15.120
The fact that countries like China, the U.S.,
Italy, and Germany, have this problem, is

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:16.960
an otherwise good sign.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:23.150
Dangerously low fertility is actually a side-effect
of many good things: increased education,

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:25.410
opportunities for women, and healthcare.

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:28.650
It’s a no-kidding first-world problem.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:34.169
There are many ways to offset the damage,
you can increase productivity, taxes, immigration

00:08:34.169 --> 00:08:35.919
and/or fertility,

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:40.399
But it’s hard to find a solution that doesn’t
come with its own set of problems.

00:08:40.399 --> 00:08:44.550
Many countries, for example, now offer incentives
for having children.

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:49.949
One of the most generous is Sweden, where
couples have the right to 480 days of paid

00:08:49.949 --> 00:08:52.339
maternity leave PER child.

00:08:52.339 --> 00:08:53.339
The downside?

00:08:53.339 --> 00:08:57.850
Employers are more hesitant to hire young
women, who are far more likely to take those

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.079
days off.

00:08:59.079 --> 00:09:03.569
And it doesn’t help that, even adjusted
for inflation, the cost of raising a child

00:09:03.569 --> 00:09:05.759
has risen for decades.

00:09:05.759 --> 00:09:08.370
Babies just can’t compete with dogs.

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:13.390
China has already gone from issuing fines
for second children to issuing checks, but

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:16.100
people just don’t seem to want them.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:21.399
This paper predicts the new two-child policy
will only increase China’s population from

00:09:21.399 --> 00:09:25.920
1.4 to 1.45 billion in 2029.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:30.800
Because a person’s ideal family size is
largely determined by their own, two generations

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:34.209
of Chinese now see one child as the norm.

00:09:34.209 --> 00:09:39.369
Plus, young people are pressured to work longer
and harder to keep up with the rising taxes

00:09:39.369 --> 00:09:42.110
needed to support the older population.

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:45.279
None of this means China can’t come up with
a solution,

00:09:45.279 --> 00:09:47.980
In fact, it has a few things going for it:

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:52.189
As people move to the city, they’ll become
bigger contributors to the economy,

00:09:52.189 --> 00:09:57.440
And today’s young workers are far better
educated than those they’re replacing - 11

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:59.639
years of schooling compared to just 6.

00:09:59.639 --> 00:10:03.989
There’s also the bigger trend towards an
automation-based economy which doesn’t rely

00:10:03.989 --> 00:10:06.139
on a such a large number of workers.

00:10:06.139 --> 00:10:09.050
But that too, has the potential for chaos.

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:15.379
And even if it does manage to increase fertility,
remember that demographic changes are slow.

00:10:15.379 --> 00:10:19.820
Children born today won’t start contributing
for at least 18 years.

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:24.350
Whatever the outcome, it’ll define China’s
role in the 21st century.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:30.610
The One-Child Policy will test China’s national
security… just as your One-Password Policy

00:10:30.610 --> 00:10:32.339
could threaten your security.

